    {
      "id": "attribution",
      "type": "layout.comment",
      "name": "Original timed radiance controls - MIT",
      "description": "Original MIT synthetic radiance scenes, PNGs, generator, projects and independent support truth by OpenCVDataflowGUI contributors. No customer/third-party images. Linear codes are radiance*128*exposure seconds; durations are 0.25, 0.5, 1.0. Motion puts a 12x10 part at three positions. Long exposure clips the third part; default clipping bounds still detect the contradiction. Ignore clipping is an explicit weaker comparison. Missing saturates an 8x8 region in every frame. Aligned applies known shifts and native MTB restores the reference grid; support metadata excludes alignment padding. Image is zero outside measured support. Display/filmstrip fills are reference-only review, not measured HDR. Strict intentionally fails 100% coverage and keeps diagnostics. Units are arbitrary response per second, not absolute radiometry or physical motion proof. See README.",
